# Plan

This is the board — the team's work on the workbook, as data. It proves a claim that
sounds wrong at first: **declarative is not immutable.** The same document you read
is the one the agents write back into; tasks move from `:todo` to `:done` *in this
file*. The document is the database.

A task is a value of the canonical `Task` struct — a record whose field types are
inferred from the defaults. No DSL, no schema.

server :board do
  defmodule Task do
    defstruct title: "", status: :todo, owner: nil, ref: nil
  end

  @statuses [:todo, :doing, :done]
end

`data` declares typed values and renders by default, so this list *is* the board on
the page. `owner` must resolve to a real person or agent; `ref` points at the unit
the task is about — both checked at the weave gate.

server data :tasks, of: Task, from: values([
  %Task{title: "Wire enrich → CRM API",       status: :done,  owner: "ana", ref: :enrich},
  %Task{title: "Tune scoring weights",         status: :doing, owner: "ana", ref: :score},
  %Task{title: "Forecast next-quarter pipe",   status: :todo,  owner: nil,   ref: :forecast},
  %Task{title: "Draft outreach for hot leads", status: :todo,  owner: nil,   ref: :analyst}
])

Two tasks have no owner — they're unclaimed. Next: how agents claim work without
trampling each other ([[Claim, lock & fence]]), and a [[A full harness]] in one file.
